<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<lint>
    <!--
      Both checks below compare the pinned versions against whatever is newest
      *today*, so their result changes without the source changing — one of them
      (NewerVersionAvailable) even reaches out to the network to do it. That
      makes a lint run non-reproducible, which is the one property a gate needs.

      Dependency freshness is handled deliberately instead: the versions live in
      gradle/libs.versions.toml with the constraints that bind them, and bumping
      them is a change you make and re-verify with `./gradlew assembleDebug`.
    -->
    <issue id="GradleDependency" severity="ignore" />
    <issue id="NewerVersionAvailable" severity="ignore" />

    <!--
      ObsoleteSdkInt fires on res/mipmap-anydpi-v26/ and advises merging it into
      an unqualified `mipmap-anydpi`, since minSdk 30 already implies v26.

      Do not take that advice: it was tried and it breaks the build. <adaptive-icon>
      is a v26 element, so in an unqualified folder aapt2 does not resolve the
      reference and linking fails with

          AAPT: error: resource mipmap/ic_launcher
          (aka com.iferoporefi.myloyaltycards:mipmap/ic_launcher) not found

      The -v26 qualifier is load-bearing regardless of minSdk.
    -->
    <issue id="ObsoleteSdkInt" severity="ignore">
        <ignore path="src/main/res/mipmap-anydpi-v26" />
    </issue>

    <!--
      DataExtractionRules asks for an `android:fullBackupContent` resource
      alongside `android:dataExtractionRules`, because the latter is ignored
      below Android 12 and minSdk here is 30.

      It is moot for this app: `android:allowBackup="false"` already disables
      backup and restore outright on Android 11, and `fullBackupContent` is only
      consulted when a backup actually runs. Adding the file would be dead config
      that implies backup is enabled when it is not. Android 12+ is covered by
      data_extraction_rules.xml, which excludes root from cloud backup AND from
      device-to-device transfer.
    -->
    <issue id="DataExtractionRules" severity="ignore" />

    <!--
      MonochromeLauncherIcon is deliberately NOT suppressed, and it no longer
      fires: Story 21.4 added the <monochrome> layer, so the check is SATISFIED
      rather than silenced. The asset it used to want is generated now, at all
      four densities, by scripts/build-brand-icons.mjs — see README.md
      § Launcher icon.

      It is promoted to FATAL by Story 16.43, and that promotion is what turns
      it into a gate. Lint's default severity here is WARNING, `abortOnError`
      fails the build on ERRORS only, and this module configures no `lint {}`
      block — so as a warning it exits 0 and gates nothing. Satisfying it once
      and regressing later would have been invisible.

      FATAL rather than ERROR, and the difference is not cosmetic. `error` gates
      only the job that explicitly runs a lint task, which is `wear-os-build.yml`
      alone. `fatal` ALSO gates `lintVitalRelease`, which AGP already wires into
      `bundleRelease` — and `bundleRelease` is what `fastlane`'s
      `build_wear_bundle!` invokes for every release pipeline. Those pipelines run
      no lint task of their own, so under `error` a regression could still ship.
      Measured, with the <monochrome> layer removed:

        lintVitalRelease, severity=error  ->  exit 0   (release path MISSES it)
        lintVitalRelease, severity=fatal  ->  exit 1   (release path CATCHES it)
        lintDebug,        severity=fatal  ->  exit 1
        both tasks, layer present, fatal  ->  exit 0

      Equally narrow — still one named issue — and strictly wider coverage.

      ⚠️ RESIDUAL RISK this mechanism cannot close: nothing protects this line
      itself. A later `lint { abortOnError = false }`, a `baseline` file, a
      `checkOnly` list, or simply editing `severity` back to `warning` would
      defeat the gate while `wear-os-build.yml` still runs and still shows green.
      There is no `lint {}` block and no baseline in this module today (verified),
      and `.gradle.kts`/`lint.xml` are invisible to eslint, jest and prettier — so
      a human reviewer is the only thing that would notice.
    -->
    <issue id="MonochromeLauncherIcon" severity="fatal" />

    <!--
      AndroidGradlePluginVersion is the THIRD member of the non-reproducible
      family above, and it is deliberately NOT suppressed — which is the
      opposite of what you would expect from that comment, so here is why.

      Suppression is only needed for a check that can FAIL a build. This one is
      a warning, warnings do not fail, and the promotion above is targeted at a
      single issue rather than a blanket `warningsAsErrors`. So it costs nothing
      but a line in the report, and an `ignore` for it would be dead config of
      exactly the kind the DataExtractionRules note above argues against.

      ⛔ IT MUST BE SUPPRESSED FIRST if anyone ever reaches for
      `warningsAsErrors` or `lint { abortOnError }` over the whole ruleset.
      Then it can fail a build, and it consults the NETWORK for the newest
      published Gradle/AGP version — so the job would go red on a day nobody
      touched the repository.
    -->
</lint>
